Scaling: SDD

Purpose:
  Define scaling rules for all system tiers. Scaling is the capacity
  management dimension of architecture — how the system handles growth
  in users, traffic, and data. Scaling decisions are driven by
  ProjectScale (../levels.sdd) and must be validated against actual
  load, not projected maximums.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  Horizontal scaling
  Vertical scaling
  Auto-scaling
  Database scaling
  Cache scaling
  Queue scaling
  CDN scaling
  Load balancing
  Scaling triggers
  Anti-patterns

Principles:
  Scale horizontally before vertically.
  Scale stateless layers before stateful layers.
  Cache before adding more compute.
  Read replicas before sharding.
  Measure before scaling.

VerticalScaling:

  description: Increase capacity of a single node (CPU, RAM, disk)
  when_to_use:
    - low scale (L0-L1)
    - simple deployment
    - temporary traffic spikes
    - legacy systems
  pros:
    - simple
    - no architecture changes
    - fast to implement
  cons:
    - single point of failure
    - hard limits
    - expensive at high end
    - downtime during upgrade
  trigger:
    - CPU consistently > 70%
    - RAM consistently > 80%
    - response time degradation
  limit:
    - vertical scaling has physical limits
    - beyond limits, horizontal is mandatory

HorizontalScaling:

  description: Add more nodes to distribute load
  when_to_use:
    - L2 and above
    - stateless services
    - high traffic
    - HA requirements
  pros:
    - near-infinite scale
    - fault tolerance
    - cost effective at scale
  cons:
    - architectural complexity
    - state management challenges
    - network overhead
  trigger:
    - CPU consistently > 60% across nodes
    - request queue depth growing
    - response time p95 increasing
  requirement:
    - application MUST be stateless or externalize state
    - session state MUST be in shared cache or DB
    - file uploads MUST go to shared storage

AutoScaling:

  description: Automatic scaling based on metrics
  when_to_use:
    - variable traffic patterns
    - L2 and above
    - cloud infrastructure
  metrics:
    - CPU utilization (target: 60-70%)
    - request count per second
    - queue depth
    - memory utilization
    - custom business metrics
  scaling_policy:
    - scale_up_when: metric > threshold for 2 consecutive minutes
    - scale_down_when: metric < threshold for 5 consecutive minutes
    - cooldown: 2 minutes after scaling action
    - max_nodes: defined per tier
    - min_nodes: at least 2 for HA
  anti_patterns:
    - scaling on transient spikes (use buffer/cache instead)
    - scaling too aggressively (cost)
    - scaling too slowly (performance)

DatabaseScaling:

  ReadReplicas:
    description: Replicate primary DB to read-only replicas
    when_to_use:
      - read-heavy workloads (read:write > 10:1)
      - reporting separated from OLTP
      - L2 and above
    routing:
      - writes -> primary
      - reads -> replica pool (round-robin or least-connections)
    limitations:
      - replication lag (eventual consistency)
      - replica count limits
      - failover complexity
    trigger:
      - primary CPU > 70%
      - read latency p95 > target
      - connection pool exhaustion on primary

  Sharding:
    description: Partition data across multiple database instances
    when_to_use:
      - data volume exceeds single node capacity
      - write throughput exceeds single node capacity
      - L3-L4 and above
    shard_key:
      - MUST be chosen carefully (immutable, evenly distributed)
      - MUST support all query patterns
      - cross-shard queries are expensive
    anti_patterns:
      - sharding before exhausting other options
      - uneven shard distribution
      - shard key changes requiring re-sharding
    trigger:
      - single DB write throughput maxed
      - data size approaching storage limit
      - single region latency unacceptable

  Partitioning:
    description: Split table data within a single database
    when_to_use:
      - large tables (100M+ rows)
      - time-series data
      - multi-tenant data
    strategies:
      - range: by date, ID range
      - hash: by key hash
      - list: by discrete values
    benefit:
      - query performance on subset
      - maintenance per partition
      - archive old partitions

CacheScaling:

  description: Scale caching layers to reduce DB load
  layers:
    - CDN: static assets, edge caching
    - reverse proxy cache: API responses
    - application cache: hot data in memory
    - distributed cache: shared session/state
  scaling_triggers:
    - cache hit rate < 90%
    - cache memory utilization > 80%
    - eviction rate increasing
  anti_patterns:
    - caching without invalidation strategy
    - caching everything (diminishing returns)
    - cache as primary data store

QueueScaling:

  description: Scale message queues for async processing
  when_to_use:
    - async workflows
    - event-driven architecture
    - L2 and above
  scaling:
    - partition count based on throughput
    - consumer count based on processing time
    - dead-letter queue for failures
  trigger:
    - consumer lag increasing
    - queue depth growing
    - processing time exceeding SLA

CDN:

  description: Content Delivery Network for static assets
  when_to_use:
    - global user base
    - static assets (images, JS, CSS, video)
    - L1 and above
  benefits:
    - reduced origin load
    - lower latency for users
    - DDoS protection
  configuration:
    - cache TTL based on asset type
    - cache invalidation on deploy
    - origin shield for cache hit ratio

LoadBalancing:

  description: Distribute traffic across healthy nodes
  types:
    - L4: transport layer (TCP/UDP)
    - L7: application layer (HTTP/HTTPS)
  algorithms:
    - round-robin: simple, equal capacity nodes
    - least-connections: variable request duration
    - weighted: heterogeneous node capacity
    - ip-hash: session affinity (avoid when possible)
  health_checks:
    - active: periodic health probe
    - passive: monitor response errors/timeouts
  anti_patterns:
    - load balancer as single point of failure (use multiple)
    - sticky sessions without need (hinders scaling)
    - ignoring health checks

ScalingTriggers:

  compute:
    - CPU > 70% for 2 minutes
    - memory > 80% for 2 minutes
    - request queue depth growing
    - p95 latency increasing

  database:
    - connection pool > 80%
    - query time p95 > target
    - replication lag > threshold
    - storage > 80%

  cache:
    - hit rate < 90%
    - memory > 80%
    - eviction rate increasing

  queue:
    - consumer lag > threshold
    - queue depth growing
    - processing time > SLA

AntiPatterns:

  PrematureScaling:
    description: Scaling before measuring actual load
    rule: MUST measure for at least one full load cycle before scaling

  VerticalOnly:
    description: Relying only on vertical scaling
    rule: horizontal scaling MUST be planned before vertical limits

  Overprovisioning:
    description: Provisioning for theoretical maximum
    rule: scale to measured needs with 20% headroom

  Underprovisioning:
    description: Insufficient capacity for current load
    rule: capacity planning MUST include growth projection

  ScalingWithoutMonitoring:
    description: Scaling without metrics
    rule: all scaling decisions MUST be metric-driven

  CacheWithoutStrategy:
    description: Caching without invalidation/expiration
    rule: every cache MUST have explicit TTL and invalidation

  ShardingTooEarly:
    description: Sharding before exhausting other options
    rule: MUST optimize queries, add replicas, use partitioning before sharding

  IgnoringState:
    description: Scaling stateless without addressing stateful bottlenecks
    rule: identify and scale the slowest tier first

  SingleAZ:
    description: Running critical tiers in single availability zone
    rule: production MUST span multiple AZs for HA

